  <main>
    <p class="intro-p">This document is the blueprint you build from and defend in your viva. It fixes one clean cryptographic scheme, states exactly who is trusted and who is not, and is honest about what the design leaks — because a project that <em class="term">knows its own weaknesses</em> scores higher than one that pretends to have none. Read it once end-to-end before writing any code.</p>

    <!-- 1 -->
    <section id="scope">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 01</div>
      <h2>Requirements &amp; scope</h2>
      <p>The problem SSE solves is a real tension. Encrypting files before uploading them to a cloud protects your data if the provider is breached or curious — but ordinary encryption also destroys your ability to search, because the server sees only random-looking bytes. <strong>Searchable Symmetric Encryption</strong> restores search over encrypted data: the client builds a special encrypted index, and the server can answer "which encrypted files contain keyword <code>w</code>?" without ever learning <code>w</code> or the file contents.</p>
      <p>For a 30-mark project the winning move is <strong>depth over breadth</strong>: implement one well-understood scheme correctly, measure it, and analyse its leakage precisely. We therefore scope the project tightly and defend that scope deliberately.</p>

      <div class="twrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Scope — in and out</caption>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:34%">Capability</th><th style="width:14%">Status</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Client-side file encryption (AES-256-GCM)</td><td><span class="tag t-trust">In</span></td><td>Confidentiality + integrity of stored files.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Single exact-keyword search over encrypted index</td><td><span class="tag t-trust">In</span></td><td>The core contribution; well-understood, correct, defensible.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Encrypted filenames &amp; encrypted document IDs</td><td><span class="tag t-trust">In</span></td><td>Closes obvious metadata leaks the naïve design misses.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Honest leakage analysis + live "what the cloud sees" demo</td><td><span class="tag t-trust">In</span></td><td>Where most of the marks and viva credibility live.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Multi-keyword / boolean / ranked search</td><td><span class="tag t-untrust">Out</span></td><td>Adds real complexity to tokens and leakage; risks overreach. Named as future work.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dynamic updates with <em>forward privacy</em></td><td><span class="tag t-untrust">Out</span></td><td>Static index only. We add files by rebuilding; we do not claim forward privacy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Defence against a <em>malicious</em> (tampering) server; ORAM</td><td><span class="tag t-untrust">Out</span></td><td>Out of the honest-but-curious model. Named as future work, not implemented.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="note decide"><span class="label">Design decision</span><p>We deliberately keep the scheme <strong>static and single-keyword</strong>. This is not a shortcut — it is the standard teaching construction (Cash et al.'s Π<sub>bas</sub>), it has a precise, citable leakage profile, and you can implement and explain every line of it. An examiner rewards a correct simple scheme far more than a half-working complex one.</p></div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <section id="roles">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 02</div>
      <h2>Client vs. simulated cloud — who is trusted</h2>
      <p>The single most important idea to get right — and the first thing you will be asked in the viva — is the <strong>trust boundary</strong>. Everything that touches a key or a plaintext lives on the <em class="term">trusted client</em>. The <em class="term">simulated cloud</em> is treated as an adversary that stores your data and honestly runs the protocol, but reads everything it can. Only ciphertext and opaque tokens ever cross the boundary.</p>
      <p>To keep all cryptography in Python (your chosen stack) while still modelling a genuine client↔server split, we run <strong>two separate Flask processes</strong>. The browser UI is a thin shell over the trusted client; the cloud is a different process on a different port that only ever receives ciphertext.</p>

      <div class="twrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Responsibility split</caption>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:32%">Trusted client <span class="tag t-trust">holds keys</span></th><th style="width:32%">Simulated cloud <span class="tag t-untrust">zero keys</span></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Derives all keys from the password (KDF).</td><td>Stores encrypted file blobs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Extracts &amp; normalises keywords from files.</td><td>Stores the encrypted inverted index (label → value dictionary).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Encrypts files with AES-256-GCM.</td><td>Runs the search loop over <em>labels</em> it cannot interpret.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Builds the encrypted index; generates search tokens.</td><td>Returns matching encrypted IDs / blobs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Decrypts search results and downloaded files.</td><td>Never decrypts, never sees a keyword, filename, or plaintext.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="note"><span class="label">Why two processes matters for marks</span><p>It lets you literally open the cloud's database in the demo and show it contains nothing but random bytes and opaque labels. A design where "client" and "server" are just two functions in one script cannot make that argument convincingly.</p></div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <section id="architecture">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 03</div>
      <h2>System architecture</h2>
      <p>Three tiers, one boundary. The browser renders the UI and never does cryptography itself; it calls the local trusted-client service. That service performs every cryptographic operation and speaks to the untrusted cloud service purely in ciphertext and tokens.</p>

