      <dl class="kv">
        <dt>K_file</dt><dd>AES-256-GCM key that encrypts file contents <em>and</em> filenames. A fresh random 96-bit nonce per encryption.</dd>
        <dt>K_index</dt><dd>The PRF master key. It is <strong>never sent</strong>; as implemented, only <code>K1</code> — the narrower of the two per-keyword keys, and only for keywords actually searched — ever crosses to the cloud. <code>K2</code> never leaves the client at all (see Section 06).</dd>
        <dt>K1 = F(K_index, 1‖w)</dt><dd>Per-keyword <em>label</em> key — generates the dictionary labels for keyword <code>w</code>. This is the only per-keyword key sent to the cloud, as a search token.</dd>
        <dt>K2 = F(K_index, 2‖w)</dt><dd>Per-keyword <em>value</em> key — encrypts the document IDs stored under those labels. Derived and used entirely client-side, both at index build and at result decryption; never transmitted.</dd>
        <dt>kdf_verifier</dt><dd>Not a key — a fixed plaintext constant (<code>b"vault-verifier:v1"</code>) encrypted under <code>K_index</code> with AES-GCM, stored alongside <code>salt</code> in the cloud's <code>meta</code> table. Minted once, on a vault's first-ever unlock — and only when both KDF rows are absent <em>and</em> the cloud's <code>documents</code> table is empty (Section 07·A); every later unlock must decrypt it successfully before <code>K_index</code> is trusted for anything else (Section 07·A, Section 09).</dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="note risk"><span class="label">Trap to avoid</span><p>Never store the master key or any sub-key to disk "to save re-deriving it". The whole security argument is that the client holds nothing at rest except a non-secret salt. If a key is written down, a stolen laptop breaks everything Argon2id was protecting.</p></div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 -->
    <section id="index">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 05</div>
      <h2>Searchable index design</h2>
      <p>The index is an <strong>encrypted inverted index</strong>: conceptually "keyword → list of documents containing it", but stored so the server can look entries up without knowing any keyword. We use the Π<sub>bas</sub> construction (Cash, Jaeger, Jarecki, Jutla, Krawczyk, Roşu, Steiner, 2014) — the canonical, provably-secure, easy-to-implement static SSE scheme.</p>
      <p>The whole index is one flat dictionary <code>D</code> of <code>label → value</code> pairs. There is no visible grouping by keyword: entries for different keywords are indistinguishable random-looking bytes sitting in the same table.</p>

      <h3>How the index is built (client side)</h3>
      <pre><span class="c"># For every keyword w and its matching documents id[0..c-1]:</span>
K1 = <b>F</b>(K_index, <span class="s">1</span> ‖ w)        <span class="c"># label key  (F = HMAC-SHA256)</span>
K2 = <b>F</b>(K_index, <span class="s">2</span> ‖ w)        <span class="c"># value key</span>
<span class="k">for</span> counter, id <span class="k">in</span> enumerate(matching_docs):
    label = <b>F</b>(K1, counter)         <span class="c"># 0,1,2,... deterministic per keyword</span>
    value = <b>AES_GCM_Enc</b>(K2, id)     <span class="c"># encrypted document identifier</span>
    D[label] = value                <span class="c"># insert into the flat dictionary</span></pre>

