    <!-- 6 -->
    <section id="tokens">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 06</div>
      <h2>Search-token generation</h2>
      <p>A <strong>search token</strong> is the minimum secret material the server needs to find one keyword's entries — and nothing more. For keyword <code>w</code> the client computes both per-keyword keys, but — as implemented — sends only <code>K1</code>:</p>
      <pre><span class="c"># client, at search time</span>
w   = tokenize(query)                 <span class="c"># same normaliser as indexing</span>
K1  = <b>F</b>(K_index, <span class="s">1</span> ‖ w)
K2  = <b>F</b>(K_index, <span class="s">2</span> ‖ w)             <span class="c"># derived, but kept client-side</span>
token = K1                            <span class="c"># only K1 crosses to the cloud</span></pre>
      <p>Because <code>F</code> is deterministic, the token for a given word is always the same. That is what makes search work — and, honestly, it is also the scheme's main leak (the server can tell when you search the same word twice). We name that in the leakage section rather than hiding it.</p>
      <p>The server uses the token to regenerate labels one by one and stops at the first miss:</p>
      <pre><span class="c"># cloud, honest-but-curious — no keys, only the token</span>
results = []
counter = 0
<span class="k">while</span> True:
    label = <b>F</b>(K1, counter)
    <span class="k">if</span> label <span class="k">in</span> D:
        results.append(D[label])      <span class="c"># still an encrypted id</span>
        counter += 1
    <span class="k">else</span>:
        <span class="k">break</span>                         <span class="c"># contiguous counters → clean stop</span>
<span class="k">return</span> results                       <span class="c"># client decrypts each with K2, derived locally</span></pre>
      <div class="note decide"><span class="label">Deviation from the original design — K2 never leaves the client</span><p>The design above assumed a token of <code>(K1, K2)</code>. <code>client/search.py</code> implements a deliberately tighter version: only <code>K1</code> is ever sent. The cloud's search loop only ever regenerates labels with <code>K1</code> — it never decrypts a value, so it never needed <code>K2</code> in the first place. <code>K2</code> is derived fresh, client-side, at the moment a result needs decrypting, from the same <code>(K_index, keyword)</code> pair that produced <code>K1</code> a line earlier — there is nothing for it to do on the wire. Sending it anyway would have widened, for zero functional benefit, this project's standing "zero keys ever cross to the cloud" invariant. This is flagged rather than silently changed: reverting to literal <code>(K1, K2)</code> is a one-line change in <code>build_search_token()</code> if strict fidelity to the original pseudocode is ever preferred. It does not change the leakage profile in Section 09 — the server still recognises repeat searches for the same word from <code>K1</code> alone.</p></div>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 -->
    <section id="workflow">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 07</div>
      <h2>End-to-end cryptographic workflow</h2>
      <p>Four flows cover the whole system. Follow them in order when you demo.</p>

      <h3>A · Unlock (key setup)</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><h4>Enter password</h4><p>Client fetches the stored <code>salt</code> and <code>kdf_verifier</code> from the cloud (neither is secret). Exactly one branch is allowed to mint both from scratch, and it is guarded by <em>two</em> conditions rather than one: both rows must be absent <em>and</em> the cloud must currently hold no documents. Absence of both rows is not by itself evidence of a fresh vault — an untrusted cloud, an operator <code>DELETE</code>, or a partially restored backup can produce exactly that state on a vault full of data.</p></li>
        <li><h4>Derive keys</h4><p>Argon2id(password, salt) → Master Key; HKDF splits it into <code>K_file</code> and <code>K_index</code>, held in memory only.</p></li>
        <li><h4>Verify, fail closed <span class="tag t-untrust">H1/H2 · F1 fix</span></h4><p>Unlock resolves to exactly one of four outcomes, and only the first two produce keys:</p>
        <ul class="unlock-cases">
          <li><strong>Both rows present, verifier decrypts → unlocked (200).</strong> <code>AES-GCM-Dec(K_index, kdf_verifier)</code> succeeds, so the derived <code>K_index</code> is the one this vault was created with.</li>
          <li><strong>Both rows absent <em>and</em> no documents stored → bootstrap (200).</strong> A genuinely fresh vault: mint a random <code>salt</code>, derive the keys, encrypt the verifier constant under <code>K_index</code>, and write both rows. This is the only branch in the whole application that creates a vault identity.</li>
          <li><strong>Verifier fails to decrypt → refused (401).</strong> The wrong password, or a substituted/corrupted <code>salt</code> or verifier — caught here, <em>before</em> the derived keys ever reach an upload or index rebuild.</li>
          <li><strong>KDF metadata incomplete → refused (409).</strong> Two distinct cases, same fail-closed answer. <em>Exactly one</em> of <code>salt</code>/<code>kdf_verifier</code> present: a deleted row, a partial write, tampering — never a fresh vault. <em>Both</em> absent while the <code>documents</code> table is non-empty: the vault plainly existed, so its KDF rows were lost or deleted. In neither case does the client guess, bootstrap, or mint a new identity over the old one, and in neither case is a single stored byte rewritten — the encrypted documents and the existing index are left exactly as they were. The vault stays locked for <em>every</em> password, correct ones included, until its KDF metadata is restored.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The last case is fix <strong>F1</strong>, closing the residual half of H2: the original H2 fix rejected one missing row but still treated <em>both</em> missing as a fresh vault, so deleting one extra row let any password "unlock" a populated vault. Regression-tested in <code>tests/test_client_app.py::TestF1BothKdfRowsMissingRegression</code> — wrong-password refusal, encrypted documents proven byte-identical after the attempt, and the genuinely-empty-vault bootstrap still working.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <h3>B · Upload &amp; index a file</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><h4>Encrypt the file</h4><p>Random 96-bit nonce; <code>ct = AES-GCM(K_file, nonce, file)</code>. Encrypt the filename the same way. Assign a random <code>doc_id</code>. Push the blob and encrypted name to the cloud — the document is safely stored at this point regardless of what happens next.</p></li>
        <li><h4>Rebuild the whole index <span class="tag t-untrust">atomic replace</span></h4><p>Implemented as a full, static rebuild rather than an incremental patch: the client re-fetches and re-decrypts <em>every</em> document currently in the vault, re-tokenizes each with the shared tokenizer, and rebuilds dictionary <code>D</code> from scratch — <code>label = F(K1,counter)</code>, <code>value = Enc(K2, doc_id)</code> for every keyword of every document. An incremental per-file patch was rejected: since <code>counter</code> is assigned in <code>doc_id</code>-sorted order, adding one file can shift the counter (and thus the label) an <em>existing</em> document gets for a shared keyword, which an additive push would leave as orphaned stale rows.</p></li>
        <li><h4>All-or-nothing push <span class="tag t-untrust">H1 fix</span></h4><p>If any document in the corpus fails to decrypt during the rebuild — historically the reachable case was an upload made right after a wrong-password unlock — the rebuild is aborted outright and <code>replace_index()</code> is never called: the cloud's existing, complete index is left exactly as it was. Nothing is silently dropped from coverage. Combined with the fail-closed unlock in Flow A, a wrong password can no longer reach this code path at all; the abort itself remains as defence-in-depth against a genuinely corrupted document.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <h3>C · Search a keyword</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><h4>Build token</h4><p>Normalise the query, derive <code>K1</code> (and, locally, <code>K2</code> for the decryption step below); send only <code>K1</code> to the cloud as the search token (Section 06).</p></li>
        <li><h4>Cloud looks up</h4><p>Server iterates <code>F(K1,counter)</code> over <code>D</code>, returns matching encrypted IDs, stops at first miss.</p></li>
        <li><h4>Decrypt IDs</h4><p>Client derives <code>K2</code> locally and runs <code>Dec(K2, value)</code> to recover the real <code>doc_id</code> list. Any single bad/tampered value aborts the whole batch rather than silently dropping it.</p></li>
        <li><h4>Fetch &amp; open</h4><p>Client requests those blobs, AES-GCM-decrypts them (tag verified), shows the plaintext filenames and files.</p></li>
      </ol>

      <h3>D · Download / view a file</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><h4>Request blob</h4><p>By <code>doc_id</code>; cloud returns <code>nonce ‖ ct ‖ tag</code>.</p></li>
        <li><h4>Decrypt &amp; verify</h4><p><code>AES-GCM-Dec(K_file, …)</code> — if the tag fails, the file was tampered or corrupted, and decryption is rejected.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 -->
    <section id="db">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 08</div>
      <h2>Database structure</h2>
      <p>The cloud uses <strong>SQLite</strong> — zero-setup, inspectable, and perfect for a demo where you dump the tables to prove they hold only ciphertext. Three tables, all on the untrusted side. The client keeps no database; it holds keys in memory and reads the salt on unlock.</p>

      <div class="twrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Cloud schema — everything here is untrusted</caption>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Table</th><th style="width:30%">Columns</th><th>What it stores / what it leaks</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono-cell">documents</td><td class="mono-cell">doc_id (PK)<br>enc_name<br>nonce<br>ciphertext<br>tag<br>size<br>uploaded_at</td><td>Encrypted file blobs and encrypted filenames. Leaks: file count, individual ciphertext length (mitigate with padding), upload timing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono-cell">sse_index</td><td class="mono-cell">label (PK)<br>value</td><td>The flat Π<sub>bas</sub> dictionary <code>D</code>. Leaks: total number of (keyword, document) pairs = row count. Individual rows reveal nothing.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono-cell">meta</td><td class="mono-cell">key (PK)<br>value</td><td>Non-secret parameters, one row per key: <code>kdf_salt</code> (16 random bytes) and <code>kdf_verifier</code> (AES-GCM encryption of a fixed constant under <code>K_index</code> — see Section 04/07·A). Both are required together; the client refuses to unlock if only one is present, and equally refuses if <em>both</em> are missing while the <code>documents</code> table still holds rows (Section 07·A). The <code>documents</code> table is what distinguishes a fresh vault from a stripped one. Neither reveals <code>K_index</code> or the password — the verifier is only ever decrypted, never derived from.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="note"><span class="label">Deliberately absent</span><p>There is no <code>keywords</code> table, no <code>filename</code> column in plaintext, and no user-key column anywhere. If a reviewer can point at a column and read something meaningful, the design has failed — so keep the schema this bare. <code>kdf_verifier</code> does not weaken this: it is ciphertext, indistinguishable from random bytes to the cloud, and exists purely so the client can refuse to trust a password it never should have.</p></div>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 -->
    <section id="threat">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 09</div>
      <h2>Security model &amp; information leakage</h2>
      <p>State the model precisely — vague threat models lose marks. This project defends against an <strong>honest-but-curious (semi-honest) cloud</strong>: it stores your data, runs the protocol faithfully, but analyses everything it can see to learn about your files and queries. The client is trusted; transport is protected by TLS (which hides data in transit — SSE is what protects it <em>at rest</em> and hides <em>what you search</em>).</p>

      <h4>Cryptographic assumptions</h4>
      <p>Security rests on standard, citable assumptions: HMAC-SHA256 is a secure pseudorandom function (labels and tokens are unpredictable); AES-256-GCM is IND-CPA secure and provides authenticated encryption (confidentiality + tamper detection); Argon2id resists offline password guessing.</p>

      <h4>What the design leaks — stated honestly</h4>
      <div class="twrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Leakage profile of Π<sub>bas</sub> — as implemented, evidence-tested in Phase 6</caption>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Leakage</th><th style="width:20%">When</th><th>What the server learns / mitigation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Setup size</strong></td><td>At upload</td><td>Number of files and total keyword-document pairs (row counts). Unavoidable in this scheme; can be blurred by padding <code>D</code> with dummy entries.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>File length</strong></td><td>At upload</td><td>Each ciphertext's size ≈ plaintext size. Mitigate by padding files to size buckets.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Search pattern</strong></td><td>At search</td><td>Whether two queries are for the <em>same</em> keyword — the <code>K1</code> search token is byte-identical across repeats of the same word (Section 06). Fundamental to Π<sub>bas</sub>; hiding it needs ORAM-style machinery (out of scope). Demonstrated live as D2 in the Phase-6 evidence suite.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Result volume</strong></td><td>At search</td><td>How <em>many</em> documents a query matched — the cloud's own <code>/search</code> response carries <code>count</code> explicitly — plus the total number of (keyword, document) postings in the whole vault, readable at any time as a bare <code>COUNT(*)</code> on <code>sse_index</code> with no decryption. A volume signal only: it says how many, never which. Demonstrated live as D3a.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Access pattern</strong></td><td>At search</td><td>Which specific <code>doc_id</code>s (as opaque handles) a query matched, and co-occurrence across queries — a distinct and strictly stronger signal than volume above. Because <code>api_search()</code> resolves each result's filename by fetching that document, one search produces the literal, cloud-visible sequence <code>POST /search</code> followed by exactly <code>count</code> × <code>GET /documents/&lt;doc_id&gt;</code>, naming the matched handles in the URL path. Repeating a search reproduces an identical access pattern; a different keyword produces a distinguishable one — linkage the cloud can read from the fetches alone, independently of D2's token equality. Amplified beyond what Π<sub>bas</sub> requires by that eager per-result fetch (audit M4, open). Demonstrated live as D3b.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Static-rebuild cost</strong></td><td>At every upload</td><td>Because the index is a full atomic rebuild rather than an incremental patch (Section 07·B), the cloud observes the client re-fetching every stored document on every single upload — the per-upload GET count grows 1→2→3… Confirms the design's own static-index cost is observable, not just theoretical. Demonstrated live as D7.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>No forward privacy across rebuilds</strong></td><td>Across uploads</td><td>A <code>K1</code> token captured once for a keyword stays valid against that keyword's index rows even after later rebuilds add new matching documents — replaying an old captured token still returns a growing match count. Named as future work in Section 01/10, not mitigated. Demonstrated live as D8 (captures a token, triggers a rebuild via a second upload, replays the old token directly against the cloud).</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="note risk"><span class="label">Be ready for this viva question</span><p>"Could the cloud recover my keywords?" — Yes, in principle, via <strong>leakage-abuse attacks</strong> (e.g. the IKK and count attacks) that combine access-pattern and volume leakage with background knowledge about the corpus. This is a known, published limitation of efficient SSE, not a bug in your code. Knowing and naming it is exactly what distinguishes a strong project.</p></div>

      <h4>Fail-closed fixes from an independent audit — H1 &amp; H2 (Phase 6)</h4>
      <p>An independent adversarial security review (<code>docs/security_audit_opus_phase6_readiness.md</code>, 12 live probes against the real two-process system) found two High-severity findings in the operational layer above — not in the cryptographic core itself. Both are now closed:</p>
      <div class="twrap">
        <table>
          <caption>H1 &amp; H2 — before vs. after</caption>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Finding</th><th style="width:39%">Before the fix</th><th>After the fix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>H1</strong> — destructive index rebuild</td><td>Unlocking with a wrong password produced no error. The next upload's index rebuild silently excluded every document that failed to decrypt, then <em>destructively replaced</em> the cloud's complete index with an incomplete one — every HTTP status on the path was a success code.</td><td>Unlock itself now fails closed (see <code>kdf_verifier</code>, Section 07·A) before a wrong-password key ever reaches an upload. As defence-in-depth, the rebuild step also refuses to push a partial index for any other reason — it aborts and leaves the existing index untouched (Section 07·B).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>H2</strong> — unauthenticated KDF material</td><td>The client accepted <code>kdf_salt</code> from the untrusted cloud with no way to check it was correct. Deleting that one row made the next unlock silently mint a brand-new vault identity while reporting "unlocked".</td><td><code>kdf_verifier</code> makes password correctness checkable: a wrong password, a deleted/corrupted salt, or a deleted verifier all fail the unlock (401 or 409) instead of silently succeeding or minting a new identity.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>F1</strong> — both KDF rows deleted <span class="tag t-untrust">follow-up</span></td><td>The H2 fix above rejected <em>one</em> missing KDF row but still read <em>both</em> missing as "fresh vault". Deleting both from a populated vault therefore minted a new identity and returned <code>200 unlocked</code> for any password at all, leaving the real documents permanently unreadable behind it.</td><td>Bootstrap now requires both rows absent <em>and</em> an empty <code>documents</code> table. Both absent with documents present is refused (409) for every password; no new identity is minted and no stored byte is rewritten (Section 07·A). Found by a follow-up review of the H1/H2 fix itself and regression-tested in <code>tests/test_client_app.py::TestF1BothKdfRowsMissingRegression</code>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The fix adds no user accounts, sessions, or broader authentication concept — <code>kdf_verifier</code> is one non-secret AES-GCM ciphertext, checked at the single point (unlock) where the audit found the gap. Of the six Medium-severity findings from the same audit, <strong>four remain open</strong> and are named here rather than hidden: Argon2 parameter versioning (the cost parameters are compiled-in constants, never persisted alongside the salt); no AAD binding (nothing ties a ciphertext to the <code>doc_id</code> it is stored under, so a cloud that swaps two whole records passes every GCM tag check); silent search truncation (a <em>corrupted</em> index value aborts the batch, but a <em>withheld</em> one simply shortens the result list undetectably); and eager result fetch, now demonstrated as D3b above. A fifth, the no-forward-privacy leak, is demonstrated as D8 and accepted by scope (§01/§10). The sixth — silent index eviction of a document that fails to decrypt — was <strong>closed as a side effect of the H1 fix</strong>: the rebuild now records every failure and aborts loudly (Section 07·B) instead of quietly dropping coverage. One narrow silent skip survives it by design: a document deleted between the rebuild's listing and its fetch is passed over rather than treated as an error. Closing H1/H2 (and F1) was this project's explicitly scoped fix, not a claim that every Medium finding is resolved.</p>
      <p>All of the above — D1 through D8, and the H1/H2 regression behaviour — are proven, not just asserted: <code>tests/test_security_demonstrations.py</code> drives the real two-process system (a live <code>cloud_app.py</code> Flask server on its own port) and records the actual wire traffic and database bytes as evidence, which a <code>pytest_sessionfinish</code> hook renders into <code>docs/security_demo_report.html</code> on every test run — a dashboard built from captured evidence, not a hand-written table of claims.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 -->
    <section id="problems">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 10</div>
      <h2>Problems to catch <em>before</em> writing code</h2>
      <p>These are the failure modes that most student SSE projects ship by accident. Design them out now.</p>
      <div class="twrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:30%">Risk</th><th style="width:34%">Why it bites</th><th>Design response</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>GCM nonce reuse</td><td>Reusing a nonce with the same key under GCM is catastrophic — it can reveal plaintext and forge tags.</td><td>Fresh random 96-bit nonce per encryption; store it with the ciphertext. Never a fixed nonce.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tokenizer mismatch</td><td>Different normalisation at index vs. search → searches silently return nothing.</td><td>Single shared <code>tokenizer.py</code>; unit-test that index and search agree.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plaintext filename leak</td><td>Encrypting contents but leaving filenames readable hands the cloud your keywords for free.</td><td>Encrypt filenames with <code>K_file</code>; store only <code>enc_name</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weak password → keys</td><td>Fast KDF (plain SHA-256, low-iteration PBKDF2) lets an attacker brute-force the vault offline.</td><td>Argon2id with tuned memory/time cost; PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 high-iteration only as fallback.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Small keyword space</td><td>Deterministic tokens + a guessable keyword set enable dictionary attacks on queries.</td><td>Acknowledge as inherent; keep keyword set realistic; note padding/obfuscation as future work.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Static-only assumption</td><td>Bolting on live updates later, naïvely, breaks <em>forward privacy</em> (new files linkable to past searches).</td><td>Scope as static: to add files, rebuild the index. Do not claim forward privacy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Server result tampering</td><td>An honest-but-curious model does not cover a malicious server dropping/altering results.</td><td>Out of scope by model; AES-GCM still detects blob tampering. Name verifiable SSE as future work.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Keys written to disk</td><td>Any persisted key defeats the at-rest guarantee.</td><td>Keys in memory only; wipe on lock; persist nothing but salt + params.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

