    <section id="testing">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 13</div>
      <h2>Testing strategy</h2>
      <p>Tests are evidence you can point to in the viva. Use <code>pytest</code> and group them by claim.</p>
      <h4>Unit (the primitives are correct)</h4>
      <p>KDF determinism (same password+salt → same keys; different salt → different keys); AES-GCM encrypt→decrypt round-trip equals the original; a flipped ciphertext byte makes decryption <em>fail</em> (tamper detection); HMAC token determinism; tokenizer produces identical output for equivalent inputs.</p>
      <h4>Functional (the system is correct)</h4>
      <p>Index build then search a known keyword returns <em>exactly</em> the expected <code>doc_id</code> set; a non-existent keyword returns empty; download→decrypt reproduces the original file (hash equality).</p>
      <h4>Security demonstrations (the claims hold)</h4>
      <p>Implemented as <code>tests/test_security_demonstrations.py</code> (8 evidence-driven demonstrations, D1–D8, recorded as 10 evidence entries because D3 and D4 each split in two, against the real live two-process system) rendering <code>docs/security_demo_report.html</code> automatically on every run: dump the cloud DB and assert no plaintext keyword, filename, or file content appears (D1); show that searching the same word twice yields identical <code>K1</code> tokens (D2); separate the two search-time leaks the cloud sees — exact match count and total index size (D3a, volume) from the observable <code>POST /search</code> → <code>GET /documents/&lt;doc_id&gt;</code> sequence naming the matched handles (D3b, access pattern); show tampering <em>within</em> a stored blob is caught by the GCM tag (D4a, D4b); show a deleted/corrupted <code>kdf_salt</code> or a wrong password now fails <em>closed</em> at unlock — 409 or 401 — rather than silently succeeding (D5, D6, the H1/H2 regression from Section 09; F1's both-rows-deleted case is regression-tested in <code>tests/test_client_app.py</code> rather than in the demonstration suite); show the static rebuild's per-upload cost is observable (D7) and that a captured token survives later rebuilds (D8, no forward privacy).</p>
      <h4>Performance</h4>
      <p>The benchmark suite below, run as repeatable tests so numbers are reproducible.</p>
