    <section id="viva">
      <div class="sec-num">Section 16</div>
      <h2>Viva defence cheat-sheet</h2>
      <p>The questions an examiner reliably asks, with crisp answers you can own.</p>
      <div class="kv">
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>How can the server search without knowing the keyword?</strong> It never sees the keyword — only a per-keyword token, <code>K1</code>, derived by HMAC (as implemented, <code>K2</code> is never sent — Section 06). It regenerates deterministic labels <code>F(K1,0), F(K1,1)…</code> and looks them up; the labels are meaningless without <code>K1</code>.</dd>
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>Why symmetric and not public-key search?</strong> Single data owner searching their own data — symmetric SSE is far faster and simpler, and public-key searchable encryption (PEKS) buys nothing here while costing performance.</dd>
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>What does the server learn?</strong> Setup size, file lengths, and — per query — search pattern, result volume, and access pattern; plus, from the static-index design, that a full rebuild happens on every upload and that a captured token stays valid across rebuilds. Keep the last two apart, because an examiner will push on it: <em>volume</em> is how many documents matched (D3a), <em>access pattern</em> is which opaque <code>doc_id</code>s they were, which the cloud reads directly from the <code>GET /documents/&lt;doc_id&gt;</code> calls our own search issues right after the token (D3b). Not the keywords or contents directly. (Section 09.)</dd>
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>What happens if someone unlocks with the wrong password?</strong> Unlock fails closed: the client decrypts a stored <code>kdf_verifier</code> with the derived <code>K_index</code>, and a wrong password makes that decryption fail, returning 401 before the key is trusted for anything. This closes an audited High-severity finding (H1) where a wrong-password session used to unlock silently and then destroy the real index on the next upload — see Section 09. The follow-up question is worth rehearsing too: <em>what if the cloud deletes the KDF rows instead?</em> One row missing, or both missing while documents still exist, is refused with a 409 and nothing is rewritten; only a vault with no KDF rows <em>and</em> no documents may bootstrap a new identity (fix F1, Section 07·A).</dd>
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>Is it breakable?</strong> Confidentiality rests on AES-GCM and HMAC. The realistic threat is leakage-abuse attacks exploiting access/volume patterns with corpus knowledge — a known limitation of efficient SSE, which is why we state it rather than hide it.</dd>
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>Why is nonce handling critical?</strong> GCM nonce reuse under one key is catastrophic; we use a fresh random nonce per encryption, stored beside the ciphertext.</dd>
        <dt>Q</dt><dd><strong>What would you add with more time?</strong> Multi-keyword (conjunctive) search, forward-private dynamic updates, result padding to blunt volume leakage, verifiable SSE against a malicious server, and the remaining Medium-severity findings from the Phase-6 audit (AAD binding, Argon2 parameter versioning).</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="note decide"><span class="label">The one-line pitch</span><p>"I built encrypted cloud storage where the server can search my files by keyword but never learns the keyword, the filename, or a single byte of content — and I can show you exactly what it does and doesn't leak."</p></div>
    </section>

